Other ways -> Feature sliced development

Normal way : Layered architecture -> is a traditional software design pattern that organizes code into horizontal layers based on technical concerns rather than business features.
Classic Layered Architecture:
src/
├── components/          # All UI components
├── services/           # All API calls and business logic
├── utils/              # All utility functions
├── hooks/              # All custom hooks
├── types/              # All TypeScript types
├── store/              # All state management
└── styles/             # All styling

Feature-Sliced Design (also called Feature-Sliced Development or FSD) is a modern architectural methodology for organizing front-end code, especially in React applications. It aims to scale applications and keep the codebase clean, maintainable, and modular.
Feature-First Organization: Instead of organizing code by technical concerns (components, utils, hooks), FSD groups code by business features and user-facing functionality.
Hierarchical Structure: The architecture follows a strict hierarchy with clear dependency rules - higher layers can import from lower layers, but not vice versa.

Layer Structure
FSD defines several standard layers, from bottom to top:
App Layer: Application initialization, global providers, routing configuration.
Pages Layer: Route-level components that compose widgets and features into complete screens.
Widgets Layer: Large composite UI blocks that combine multiple features - headers, sidebars, complex forms.
Features Layer: User interactions and business features - authentication, search, shopping cart. These are complete user scenarios.
Entities Layer: Business entities and their associated logic - user profiles, products, orders. These represent core domain concepts.
Shared Layer: Contains reusable code that does not belong to any specific feature - UI components, utilities, API clients, constants, and configuration.

Within each layer, code is organized into "slices" - self-contained modules that group related functionality:

Each slice follows a consistent internal structure called "segments" like ui (components), model (state management), api (data fetching), and lib (utilities).

src/
├── app/
├── pages/
├── widgets/
├── features/
│   ├── authentication/
│   │   ├── ui/
│   │   ├── model/
│   │   ├── api/
│   │   └── index.ts
│   └── search/
├── entities/
└── shared/


